## 24. Player Skills (NEW)

The capability-progression spine that complements the content-gating XP tracks (DEX, CRIM).

### The distinction

- **XP tracks (DEX, CRIM)** gate content. They unlock new customers, new tools, new contacts. Player feels progression through "I can now do X."
- **Player skills** gate capability and access. They make moment-to-moment play feel better and slowly open up the world. Player feels progression through "I feel faster / I can carry more / I can reach places I couldn't before."

Both are slow burns. Both are felt. They complement, they don't compete.

### Visibility model

- **No involuntary numbers.** The Skyrim "+1 Lockpicking" popup is the failure mode — it turns the action into a number-collection game.
- **One-time threshold notifications.** When a skill crosses a meaningful threshold, a quiet message ("You feel quicker on your feet"). Once. Never again.
- **Optional dedicated screen.** Stardew-style. Player opens it when they're curious. Shows progress bars to next level rather than raw XP numbers. Closes. Not pushed at them.

### Leveling: Bethesda-style, gain-from-doing

Skills increase by engaging with relevant actions. Small per-action gains naturally accumulate during normal play. No grinding loopholes because gains are tied to actions that consume game time and produce game state.

Per-day caps prevent degenerate spam where applicable.

### Initial skill set

### Athletics
- XP accrues per pixel of player movement, committed via fractional accumulator.
- Speed scales linearly: `1.0 + level * 0.015`. L50 = 140 px/s (base 80).
- Capability thresholds: `vault_low` at L10, `vault_medium` at L25, `vault_high` at L50.
- Each threshold fires a one-time notification.
- XP curve: `round(20 * pow(level, 1.6))` cumulative. ~12,649 XP to reach L50.

### Vault (implemented)
- Authoring: 1-tile-thick rule in the traversal axis. Stacked vaultables are rejected.
- Vaultable tiles painted in a per-room `TileMap` named "Vaultables" with a
  `vault_tier` custom data layer (0/1/2 = low/medium/high).
- Visual fence rows above the vault row live in the world tilemap, not Vaultables.
- Input: dedicated `vault` action (spacebar).
- Detection: scan player's current tile (north-approach edge case) and one tile ahead.
- Slide tween + sprite Y-offset arc (separate tweens, additive).
- Per-tier tuning:
  - Duration: 0.4s / 0.6s / 0.9s
  - Arc height: 10px / 18px / 32px
  - XP: 5 / 10 / 20
- Landing offset (per-axis) compensates for foot-centered player origin.
- Animation `speed_scale` set so anim length matches vault duration.
- Z-sort fix: `z_index` is flipped at arc apex (t > 0.5) based on traversal direction
  to make "clearing the fence" read correctly. East/west vaults leave z_index alone.
  (Note: `y_sort_origin` does not exist on Node2D; z_index manipulation is the
  correct approach here.)
- Stamina cost: 2 per vault.

### Strength
- XP accrues on movement, multiplied by filled inventory slots.
- Inventory expands by chunks at threshold levels:
  - Start: 6 slots
  - L10/L20/L30/L40/L50: +6 slots each (capability flags `carry_12` through `carry_36`)
- `Inventory` listens to `PlayerSkills.level_up` and resizes itself on threshold;
  emits `capacity_changed` so dependent UIs (hotbar, inventory panel) rebuild.

### Lockpicking
- Flat 3 XP per successful locked car loot. (Tune after playtest — currently slow.)
- Duration reduction: `max(0.1, 1.0 - level * 0.012)`. L50 = 40% faster picks.
- No capability gates currently — slim jim + skill scaling only.
- Future complexity (gated tiers) can layer on top.

**Stealth** (defer until heat system lands)
- Trigger: small XP per second in a witness's viewport without being detected. Larger XP on successful completion of a crime undetected.
- Effect: lengthens the time it takes for a witness's "noticed" meter to fill.

### Cop pursuit vault interaction
Deferred — design intent recorded: cops can't vault medium until high pursuit aggression. Implementation waits for cop AI / heat system.

---

### Vault as the headline capability

Vaultable objects are placed throughout outdoor scenes — low fences, mid-height walls, dumpsters, rooftop ledges. Each is tagged with a tier (low / medium / high). Player can vault objects of tiers they've unlocked.

- **Low vault** (Athletics ~10): low fences, trash piles. Common throughout the map. Becomes the early "the world feels more open now" moment.
- **Medium vault** (Athletics ~25): chain-link fences, low walls. Creates chase-mechanic shortcuts. Cops can't vault medium until high pursuit aggression.
- **High vault** (Athletics ~50): walls, rooftop access. Late-game unlock for area access — gates content behind it.

Vault is committed: pressing the vault key during a vault locks the player into the animation, can't be canceled or steered. Half-second commit. Prevents spamming as evasion; creates real timing decisions in chase scenarios.

**This turns the level geometry into progression-relevant terrain.** The map literally changes as the player progresses — alleys with low fences become traversable, then chain-link cuts open new routes, then walls unlock new areas. Same trick as Metroidvania traversal, applied to an urban life sim.

### Skill gates as content authoring lever

The biggest design payoff of skills isn't the buffs — it's that **skills become a content-gating axis for events and quests**:

- An NPC opens up to high-stealth players because they assume you can keep a secret
- A specific Fence offer requires lockpicking tier 3
- A side questline involving rooftop deliveries opens at Athletics tier 3 (high vault unlock)
- A neighbor only mentions the basement burglary if your forced-entry skill is high enough to be useful

Players who skill into different things *see different games*. This is huge for content variety and replay.


### Architecture
- `PlayerSkills` autoload owns all skills via a single `SKILL_CONFIG` dict keyed by skill id.
- Adding a new skill = new config entry + trigger wiring in the source system.
- One save dict, one set of signals (`skill_changed`, `level_up`, `capability_unlocked`).
- Skills are distinct from XP tracks (DealerExperience, CriminalExperience):
  skills gate *capability* (speed, carry, traversal); XP tracks gate *content* (customers, contacts).


### Open design questions

- Skill caps: aiming for 30+ hour saves, so caps should align with that target. Tentatively each skill caps at ~50 levels with the late levels increasingly slow.
- Whether to surface skills before the player's first level-up, or wait until they cross a threshold so the screen appears as a discovery rather than a tutorial element.

